    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "reasoning": "Student correctly calculated 90 - 39 = 51 and identified option C. Finalize with clear answer, formula breakdown, and exam tip following the mandatory structure.",
      "content": "Yes, $\\theta = 51^\\circ$ because the normal is perpendicular ($90^\\circ$) to the surface of the block, meaning the angle with the normal and the angle with the surface add up to $90^\\circ$.\n\n**Final Answer:** C\n\n---\n### 📖 Formula Breakdown (How It Works):\n* **The Formula:** $\\theta_{\\text{normal}} = 90^\\circ - \\theta_{\\text{surface}}$ (or application of complementary angles and Snell's Law geometry)\n* **What the letters mean:**\n  * $\\theta_{\\text{normal}}$: The angle measured relative to the normal (perpendicular line to the interface).\n  * $\\theta_{\\text{surface}}$: The angle measured relative to the flat surface of the rectangular block.\n* **Why we use it:** Optics problems often trick you by giving angles relative to the glass surface instead of the normal. We subtract the given angle from $90^\\circ$ because the normal line is always perpendicular ($90^\\circ$) to the boundary surface.\n\n🎯 **Exam Tip:** Always double-check whether your angle in Snell's Law ($n_1 \\sin\\theta_1 = n_2 \\sin\\theta_2$) is measured from the **normal** and not the surface! Examiners frequently test this specific pitfall."
    }
  ],
  "meta": {
